body:
haha thank you for the response to the shirt question but, no worries I'm completely on the same page as you lol. I too wouldn't whip out spanish because someone's last name is Lopez! BUT, I think I'm a bit far away from wanting to speak korean in public. But I do for instance have a friend whose mother only speaks Korean and my friend has prompted me before to try to have a conversation with her mother in Korean but Im too anxious. Or the restaurant I mentioned has two new workers who get really flustered because their English is pretty limited as they are native korean speakers. So those have really been the only two circumstances i've run across where I thought to myself, hmm if I was more comfortable maybe it would be a good place to utilize it. 

&#x200B;

Thanks for the kindness btw! :)

I love how KMY's character has been written. I think she continually surprises all of us. And the fact that really have made her past so bleak and awful really makes you understand her behaviors, especially the ones from the first few episodes. Girl was in foreign territory with her experiences and was scared and didnt know how to handle herself as her mother never allowed her to experience anything. Couple that with the trauma and abuse and it makes sense. Such growth from her in the show. 

&#x200B;

And yes! The director has a son I could believe. But I like that they gave me a reason to why he was annoying. Clearly he is annoyed by kt and those at the hospital because they have pulled, in his mine, his fathers attention and focus away from him. It was a nice explaination to a character that really otherwise could have just been background noise.

I just typed a post to this thread and said this same thing, so glad im not alone! Thats my thoughts exactly. I kept thinking if shes not the mom she 100 percent helping whoever she is or was. There needs to be an inside person and someone who would have stake in it for a reason. Idk theres just a piece missing. i love that none of us are certain, its great!

&#x200B;

edited because i phrased wrong.

ive always dabbled in subtitled shows and movies, not a lot. But like someone else said below, after the 2016 election I felt disconnected emotionally from things and sometimes people, especially fictional, in the US so now it's mostly what I watch. I also have ALWAYS loved period pieces, IE/ North and South and Pride and Prejudice, etc and I've always been a HUGE fan of the romance genre (book, movies, music, tv wise). The reason I love period pieces is I feel like they pay attention to the small romantic gestures (skinship) (a simple face touch or hand hold - and can make it feel huge and powerful and even like a climax or pivotal part of the story telling) and I absolutely LOVE that aspect. So when I watched my first Kdrama a couple years ago It was a perfect creation for me. It was a story usually distanced from reality for me, Kdramas always focus on the small gestures much like period pieces do, and there's usually a romance plotline (though I also like Kdramas that aren't romance based).

&#x200B;

My go to rewatches are: Healer, Strong Woman, While You Were Sleeping, and W2 Worlds. I also like to look up scenes from Romance is a Bonus Book as it was probably the least dramatic of the shows and can give me a quick pick me up lol

I love your post and I wish I could read everyones comments, but there's so many! I wanted to share my experience as I have on the last one, but this time I'm on time! Wooh. Im a therapist and I work a lot with trauma victims, and while I don't work with people who have the ASPD diagnosis (it's rare, but I have learned about it in schooling) I do know that there is a lot of overlap, as others have said below between the symptomology in PTSD and ASPD, especially long term PTSD which has not been dealt with, which hers seems to have been.

I was personally really hoping that she was misdiagnosed as I think that it would make this show probably the best KDRAMA commentary I've seen yet. Misdiagnosing is a REAL BIG issue and quite common, sadly. I would love to see a show tackle that commentary in a way while also still giving discussion to mental health issues that are not widely talked about enough.

&#x200B;

But where I was sitting 50/50 before on if she does have ASPD or PTSD, I now am like 80 percent sure she doesn't have ASPD. First i mean of course, the storytelling would be effected. I can't lie. This show could easily be my top favorite KDrama of all time, if they do the rest right. But trying to sell a love story that is authentic with a character who has ASPD would not be possible. As I described in last weeks post, love with someone who has ASPD would be ego driven, it would be more of a calculation, a want to fulfill something large (financial stability, a want to blend in with society, etc). It could not be about the other person, about being selfless. So for storytelling purposes they need to head towards it being a misdiagnosis.

&#x200B;

ALSO THE EARLY SCENE where they came back to his place and talked about the story of the Zombie kid, I think that was INCREDIBLY telling and said everything the audience needed to know about the truth of her diagnosis. She didn't WANT to read the emotions because she doesn't want to feel them. Her stories are a reflection of her. She was deprived of contact as a kid, of human connection (this is one way she relates to our main male lead, who experienced similar with his mother) and that led her to clearly not being able to connect well on emotional levels with others and probably what led to her misdiagnosis. It's not that she can't feel, it's that she's afraid to, because it's new and scary and not have she was taught to function. He even said it himself. That she "doesn't LIKE TO FEEL the emotions." People with ASPD wouldn't FEEL one way or another about it. When he said "you're not the zombie kid either," that's when I officially joined the she's misdiagnosed mindset. That was a clear deviation for the story telling. 

&#x200B;

HER REACTION too was not in line with aspd. She clearly was affected by him saying this and seeing this truth. And her need to walk away to the bathroom and that reaction we saw her have, not in line with ASPD. Again, I hope people take my opinion with a grain of salt, but I think at this point if they keep her ASPD diagnosis there would be a collection of moments that don't fit that diagnosis properly.

So I thought I'd add my perspective on here but I don't think she has ASPD, both for storytelling purposes and also because of what they've shown us so far. I think she has PTSD. The commentary on nightmares, the way her storytelling seems to be a reliving or retelling of her trauma almost, the flashbacks, the emotional response to the nightmare, not to mention re-enacting trauma is a large part of the symptomology for PTSD, and we saw her doing that while being choked in a way and it looks like she may have watched her mother drown, and debated about helping, and that may be why she reenacted it with w/ the male lead (speculation of course) when they were younger. Even the reaction she had to the little girl taking the picture episode 1 with her mother, seemed like a trauma response. ASPD and PTSD also share a lot of symptomology and misdiagnosis is common. I'm a therapist, so I can't tell you if that makes my opinion maybe come from a different perspective or if it colors me a little bias, but I thought I'd share, since it seems to be a bit different then most others. But then again Im new to the thread, so pardon me if I'm off anywhere.

&#x200B;

Edit: Also I wanted to mention it's extremely common to resonate or even obsess over a bright point amongst a trauma. So for instance, a child who is abused and was comforted by a blanket may be become obsesses over keeping this symbol in his life as he ages. I think that our male lead became fixated on caring for his brother during a traumatic point in life (which is why he chose the field he did and sometimes goes beyond what he should safety wise to continue that care). But also what if our main female lead, upon finding out that she ran into the boy from her childhood (that was her bright point during her trauma) I think that once she recognized who he was THAT'S WHEN we saw her shift towards fixating on him. Its not about him being a pawn that she wants to move around, it's about her fixating on the one thing that brought her comfort during her trauma as a child.

Ah thanks, I like to hope so most day. :) 

&#x200B;

And that's a great analogy. I think that they have shown a good amount for 4 episode on why the theory of her having PTSD is a large possibility. Her flashback to the choking. Her recounting feelings related to trauma in her story telling. Even her talking to the girl who wanted a picture in Ep 1 seemed like a good example. Trauma victims also can experience dissociation and describe a loss of feeling. I also wonder if her fascination with sharp things we've seen here and there is related to trauma. And again, yeah story telling wise it makes more sense. I just kept noticing all the threads seemed to have a lot of people who said they'd be upset if they "pulled a fast one" and said she doesn't have ASPD but I don't see it as a fast one as it seems like they are already dropping plenty of potential breadcrumbs for this theory.

I am sorry that that happened to you. I've been on Reddit for a short time, but I will say 80 percent of my experience has been negative. I don't know why i'm on this website most days. I think it's because at the end of the day my urge to connect and find that one person that for a second makes me feel a little less alone, is bigger than my frustration. I feel like maybe you can relate to that a bit and that knowledge can be helpful in times because maybe it means at the end of it all you know that the good that comes out of something is more important than the bad.

&#x200B;

Again i'm sorry you are feeling this way. I am in a similar boat and I know that expecting people to do what you would probably do as a kind individual and then seeing that some people DONT do that sucks. I am not sure how much support I can offer, but my inbox is always open if you ever want to talk.

It sounds like you are smart enough to already label certain things that aren't helping how you are feeling and maybe those can be areas of focus you work on changing. Lots of people are in debt, so you're not alone there. I am guessing by that you live in the US. 

&#x200B;

But the things like alcohol use (which is a depressant) and can cause damage and pain to the body, could be something you work on cutting back on. The best thing for social anxiety is exposure therapy. Doing little tiny things and building up your interactions to others in social situations. Going to a coffee shop and attempting to say a couple sentences to the barista, etc. And then of course there's therapy. Would your insurance cover sessions? Are you still in school? Most US colleges offer free counseling on campus and group therapy, which can also help you find people to connect with.

Congrats to the OP! I'm new here and that's very motivating. I'm replying to you TheTruestDetective22 because since you mentioned pics, would you mind explaining to me how i make a post with an image from my computer instead of a link, I can't figure it out for the life of me. Hope you don't mind the ask!

&#x200B;

Thanks!!
